Raise MAX_PAGE_ORDER from 10 to 13 for the rv32 nommu target.

On nommu every process needs one physically *contiguous* allocation for its
whole text+data+bss+stack, so the largest block the buddy allocator can hand
out is a hard ceiling on binary size. The default MAX_PAGE_ORDER of 10 caps
that at 2^10 pages = 4 MiB, which is why this image hits an allocation cliff
long before it runs out of free memory (measured: fails at 10 MiB RAM with
3,432 kB still free but the largest free block only 512 kB).

riscv, unlike arm/arm64/powerpc, never defines CONFIG_ARCH_FORCE_MAX_ORDER in
its Kconfig, so there is no config symbol to set -- the constant has to be
changed at the source. This is the same change cnlohr's mini-rv32ima README
calls for ("You *MUST* build your kernel with MAX_ORDER set to >12 ... if you
are building your own image"), updated for the 6.8 rename of MAX_ORDER to
MAX_PAGE_ORDER.

13 gives a 32 MiB maximum block, which is the whole point: a statically linked
interpreter is several MiB of one contiguous region under bFLT.

--- a/include/linux/mmzone.h
+++ b/include/linux/mmzone.h
@@ -27,7 +27,7 @@
 
 /* Free memory management - zoned buddy allocator.  */
 #ifndef CONFIG_ARCH_FORCE_MAX_ORDER
-#define MAX_PAGE_ORDER 10
+#define MAX_PAGE_ORDER 13
 #else
 #define MAX_PAGE_ORDER CONFIG_ARCH_FORCE_MAX_ORDER
 #endif
